[110.510342, "o", "ndle the modifications as we go.\\n\\nFor example:\\n\\nprev_v = -1\\ncurrent_v = start\\nwhile current_v < n:\\n    next_v = next[current_v]\\n    if not visited[current_v]:\\n        if current_v not in non_edges.get(u, set()):\\n            visited[current_v] = True\\n            component_size +=1\\n            queue.append(current_v)\\n            # Remove current_v from the linked list\\n            if prev_v != -1:\\n                next[prev_v] = next_v\\n            if next_v < n:\\n                prev[next_v] = prev_v\\n            current_v = next_v\\n        else:\\n            prev_v = current_v\\n            current_v = next_v\\n    else:\\n        current_v = next_v\\n\\nThis way, we iterate through the linked list, handling the removal of nodes as we go.\\n\\nBut implementing this correctly is tricky.\\n\\nAnother alternative is to use a BFS approach where the next nodes are found by using the linked list structure and skipping non_edges.\\n\\nBut this is getting too vague.\\n\\nMaybe I should look for similar problems or kn"]
[110.510384, "o", "own algorithms.\\n\\nUpon checking, this problem is similar to the BFS approach for finding connected components in a complement graph. The standard solution for this problem uses a linked list to track unvisited nodes and processes nodes by iterating through the linked list and checking against the non_edges.\\n\\nHere\\'s a possible approach inspired by this solution:\\n\\n1. For each node, store the non_edges in a hash set.\\n\\n2. Maintain a linked list of unvisited nodes using next and prev arrays.\\n\\n3. Initialize the linked list with all nodes.\\n\\n4. For each unvisited node u:\\n\\n   a. Perform BFS starting from u.\\n\\n   b. For each node in the BFS queue, iterate through all nodes in the linked list of unvisited nodes.\\n\\n   c. For each such node v, if v is not in u\\'s non_edges, add v to the component, mark it as visited, and remove it from the linked list.\\n\\n   d. Add v to the BFS queue.\\n\\nThe key is step b: during processing u, iterate through all unvisited nodes and check if they are adjacent to u. This al"]
[110.51039, "o", "lows us to collect all nodes connected to u in the current component.\\n\\nBut how to iterate through all unvisited nodes efficiently.\\n\\nThe linked list approach allows this. The linked list starts at a head node, and each node has a next pointer. So, during processing u, we can iterate through the linked list, and for each node v:\\n\\n   if v is not in non_edges[u], then add to component.\\n\\nBut in code, this can be done as follows:\\n\\ncurrent = start\\nwhile current < n:\\n    next_node = next[current]\\n    if current not in non_edges[u]:\\n        # Add current to component\\n        # Remove current from linked list\\n        if prev[current] != -1:\\n            next[prev[current]] = next_node\\n        if next_node < n:\\n            prev[next_node] = prev[current]\\n        if current == start:\\n            start = next_node\\n        # Mark as visited\\n        visited[current] = True\\n        component_size +=1\\n        queue.append(current)\\n    current = next_node\\n\\nBut this would process all unvisited nodes o"]
[110.510399, "o", "nce for each u in the BFS queue.\\n\\nBut this leads to each node being processed multiple times, which is not efficient.\\n\\nThus, the correct approach is to, for each component, collect all nodes that can be reached by any node in the component through thei"]
[110.510485, "o", "r non_edges.\\n\\nWait, no. The component is built by adding all nodes that can be reached from u via edges in the original graph.\\n\\nBut in the original graph, edges are the complement of the non_edges, so two nodes are adjacent if they are not in each other\\'s non_edges.\\n\\nThus, during BFS, when processing a node u, the adjacent nodes are all unvisited nodes not in u\\'s non_edges. These nodes are added to the component and their own adjacent nodes are processed similarly.\\n\\nBut the key is that each node in the component must be connected to u via a path of nodes where each pair is connected (i.e., not in each other\\'s non_edges).\\n\\nThis is the standard BFS, but the adjacency list is generated on the fly.\\n\\nThe linked list approach allows us to iterate through all unvisited nodes each time and check if they are adjacent to the current node.\\n\\nBut how to implement this.\\n\\nAfter some research, I found that this problem is a classic example where the linked list approach is used. Here\\'s how it\\'s done:\\n\\n"]
[110.510503, "o", "1. Read the non_edges for each node.\\n\\n2. Initialize a linked list of all nodes using next and prev arrays.\\n\\n3. Initialize a visited array to track visited nodes.\\n\\n4. Iterate through each node from 0 to n-1. For each unvisited node u:\\n\\n   a. Start a new component.\\n\\n   b. Perform BFS/DFS by iterating through all unvisited nodes not in u\\'s non_edges.\\n\\n   c. For each such node v, add them to the component, mark as visited, and remove from the linked list.\\n\\nThe BFS is handled by adding all such nodes to a queue and processing them similarly.\\n\\nThis approach works because any node added to the component is connected to u via a path of nodes in the component.\\n\\nBut why does this work?\\n\\nBecause when processing u, all nodes v that are adjacent to u (i.e., not in u\\'s non_edges) are added to the component. Then, when processing v, all unvisited nodes not in v\\'s non_edges are added, and so on. This ensures that all nodes reachable from u via the original graph\\'s edges are added to the component.\\n\\n"]
[110.510607, "o", "But why does this capture all nodes in the component?\\n\\nBecause during the processing of each node in the component, we check all unvisited nodes against the current node\\'s non_edges, and add those not present. This is equivalent to traversing the BFS in the original graph, where each step explores all adjacent nodes (unvisited and not in non_edges).\\n\\nThe linked list ensures that each unvisited node is processed exactly once, and the BFS ensures that all reachable nodes are added to the component.\\n\\nThis approach should have a time complexity of O(n + m), because each node is processed once, and each non_edge is checked once per node in the BFS.\\n\\nThus, this is feasible for n up to 2e5.\\n\\nNow, the task is to implement this in Python.\\n\\nBut Python\\'s performance can be an issue for large n. So we need to optimize the code as much as possible.\\n\\nHere\\'s the plan for the code:\\n\\n- Read n and m.\\n\\n- For each of the m non_edges, add both directions to a dictionary of sets (since the graph is undirected)"]
