[110.511204, "o", "those not present are added to the component and marked as visited.\\n\\nThe key is to manage the unvisited nodes using a global set, and for each u, iterate through this set to find the adjacent nodes.\\n\\nBut given the time constraints, perhaps the correct way to proceed is to use a BFS approach with a set for unvisited nodes, and for each u, compute the set difference between the unvisited set and u\\'s non_edges.\\n\\nBut for Python, using a set for unvisited nodes and iterating through it can be time-consuming for large n.\\n\\nHowever, given that m is up to 2e5, and each non_edge is stored as a set, perhaps the following code can work:\\n\\nimport sys\\nfrom sys import stdin\\n\\ndef main():\\n    input = sys.stdin.read().split()\\n    ptr = 0\\n    n = int(input[ptr])\\n    ptr += 1\\n    m = int(input[ptr])\\n    ptr += 1\\n\\n    non_edges = [[] for _ in range(n)]\\n    for _ in range(m):\\n        x = int(input[ptr])-1\\n        ptr +=1\\n        y = int(input[ptr])-1\\n        ptr +=1\\n        non_edges[x].append(y)\\n      "]
[110.511291, "o", "  non_edges[y].append(x)\\n    # Convert to sets for O(1) lookups\\n    for i in range(n):\\n        non_edges[i] = set(non_edges[i])\\n\\n    unvisited = set(range(n))\\n    components = []\\n\\n    while unvisited:\\n        u = next(iter(unvisited))\\n        queue = [u]\\n        unvisited.remove(u)\\n        component_size = 1\\n        for node in queue:\\n            # Adjacent nodes are all unvisited nodes not in non_edges[node]\\n            adjacent = unvisited - non_edges[node]\\n            component_size += len(adjacent)\\n            queue.extend(adjacent)\\n            unvisited -= adjacent\\n        components.append(component_size)\\n\\n    components.sort()\\n    print(len(components))\\n    print(\\' \\'.join(map(str, components)))\\n\\nThis code is concise, but in Python, the line \\'unvisited -= adjacent\\' can be O(k), where k is the size of \\'adjacent\\'. The \\'extend\\' is also O(k). The BFS processes each node once.\\n\\nBut the \\'adjacent\\' set can be O(n) in size, leading to O(n^2) time in the worst case, which is "]
